// `isize` and `usize` are admissible members of an `immutable` type.
//
// They were not, and the rejection read as a compiler fault because it was one: the diagnostic said
// `Bounds` "has a mutable member `n` of type `ptrdiff_t`" while its own list of what IS allowed opened
// with "a primitive" — which `isize` is. A scalar has no interior mutability; there was never a reason.
//
// ⚠️ The cause is the one kama.ast.h warns about at the builtin-value block. `isize`/`usize` are
// APPENDED after `INT8..CHAR` precisely so the ordered spans that read it keep meaning what they meant,
// which puts the burden on every site that should ALSO cover a size type to name the two explicitly.
// `fieldTypeDeeplyImmutable` read the span and stopped. It was wrong before `0.9.136` too — back then
// the pair had no builtin value at all, so it fell through to "is there a class called `ptrdiff_t`?"
// and answered no — and the campaign that converted ~13 such sites did not reach this one. A sweep is
// not a proof; this fixture is.
//
// The guarantee itself is unchanged and still enforced, which matters more than the fix:
// tests/xfail/immutable_mutable_field.kama holds the other side, and it still rejects.

type immutable value Bounds {
    public isize lo;              // a signed size — the type every length() and index in kama is
    public usize span;            // the unsigned C-ABI peer
    public int32 tag;             // an ordinary fixed-width primitive, for contrast

    public ctor make(isize lo, usize span, int32 tag) {
        this.lo = lo;
        this.span = span;
        this.tag = tag;
    }
}

// Deep immutability is TRANSITIVE, so a type built out of the one above must be accepted for the same
// reason — this is what proves the fix reaches the fixpoint pass and not just the leaf test.
type immutable value Region {
    public Bounds inner;
    public isize stride;

    public ctor make(Bounds inner, isize stride) { this.inner = inner; this.stride = stride; }
}

fn int32 main()
{
    Bounds b = Bounds.make(lo: 4, span: 6, tag: 7);
    Region r = Region.make(inner: b, stride: 2);

    return cast<int32>(r.inner.lo)        // 4
         + cast<int32>(r.inner.span)      // 6
         + r.inner.tag                    // 7
         + cast<int32>(r.stride);         // 2   -> 19
}
